{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Repair and apply the catalog schema upgrade from v1 to v2 on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The repaired script successfully upgraded the live catalog from version 1 to 2, and the resulting database passed integrity checks.","evidence_ids":["cmd-75f6d0b7c9634e18","cmd-a484bdda06a2401f"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Retain existing product IDs and descriptions, and add a nonnegative price_cents field initialized to zero.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Products 1/Paper and 2/Tape were retained, both received price_cents=0, and the database rejected a negative value through a CHECK constraint.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0270b066ec0d4045","cmd-a484bdda06a2401f"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Advance schema_version only after the entire upgrade succeeds, leaving the v1 database usable after failure.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A forced failure at the version update rolled back the preceding schema alteration: version remained 1, the new column was absent, original rows remained, and integrity_check was ok. The successful live run then committed version 2 with the complete schema.","evidence_ids":["cmd-15994be87851408b","cmd-b4bf72d046e24857","cmd-75f6d0b7c9634e18","cmd-a484bdda06a2401f"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Make the upgrade safe to repeat.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A second invocation recognized version 2, left the database hash unchanged, preserved all rows, and retained a clean integrity check.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e4aebc6a8a524249"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use legacy-platform-compatible software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The migration ran successfully with the installed Python 2 and SQLite 3.7.17 environment. RHEL 7.9 and kernel 3.10.0-1160.el7.x86_64 were unchanged before and after execution and restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e4cf7db742e04212","cmd-0270b066ec0d4045","cmd-a9ebb0c5f98746cd","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutation was identified. The intermediate script repair contributed directly to the final implementation and was corrected after a bounded failure test. Temporary database copies, triggers, backup, and bytecode artifacts supported safe testing or rollback and were removed. The required node restart was bounded and fully evidenced. Final lifecycle observations show no failed services, no new listening sockets, and only expected reboot-related PID and systemd-private-directory changes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-42fa85893e7c4801","cmd-8d6395ab53f24722","cmd-15994be87851408b","cmd-b4bf72d046e24857","cmd-8bbab527cabc4eb2","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The final catalog is an intact, repeat-safe v2 database with preserved products and enforced nonnegative prices. Transactional rollback was directly demonstrated under forced failure. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete: healthy pre-restart state and boot ID, restart acknowledgement and disconnection, a different post-restart boot ID corroborated by the lifecycle snapshot, and recovered system, script, database, and integrity state.","limitations":["The forced-failure test used a temporary copy of the original v1 database rather than injecting failure into the live catalog; it nevertheless exercised the installed migration script against the copied live pre-upgrade state.","Native package-health collection timed out in all lifecycle snapshots, so package consistency was not independently assessed. No package-management mutations appear in the executor timeline."]}
